<section xml:id="sec-application-of-vectors-to-geometry" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Application of Vectors to Geometry</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.6 Vectors II
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Apply vectors to geometry in different situations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Using vectors to prove and solve geometric problems.
         Suggested activity: Learners use vectors to show that the line joining the midpoints of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side and half its length.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Draggable triangle with midpoints joined; the vector relationship to the third side stays true as the triangle changes. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core 2.6 (h); (i) reflect on the use of vectors placed here (paragraphs "Vectors Around Us")
         Activity: as planning notes (the midpoint theorem by vectors) | added: part (f), points one third of the way from O
         JSXGraph: fig-midpoint-theorem-interactive (assets/jsxgraph/midpoint-theorem.js), as planning notes
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/12-vectors-ii/09-application-of-vectors-to-geometry/ -->

    <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
        <ol>
            <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> how to describe a figure with two non-parallel base vectors; how parallel and collinear proofs work; when coefficients may be equated.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> prove the midpoint theorem and related results with vectors, and find where two lines in a triangle meet.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> reason about paths in a triangular plot, and reflect on where vectors are used.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
            <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners prove the midpoint theorem with vectors and check it by measuring their own triangle.</p></li>
            <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Each learner draws a different triangle. After part (e), collect results from around the class: every learner's <m>MN</m> is half of their <m>AB</m>. Use this to show the class that one vector proof covers all their triangles at once.</p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-midpoint-theorem">
        <title>The Midpoint Theorem</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Draw any triangle <m>OAB</m> and let <m>\overrightarrow{OA} = \mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{OB} = \mathbf{b}</m>. Mark <m>M</m>, the midpoint of <m>OA</m>, and <m>N</m>, the midpoint of <m>OB</m>.</p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-midpoint-theorem">
                <caption>Triangle <m>OAB</m> with <m>M</m> and <m>N</m> the midpoints of <m>OA</m> and <m>OB</m>.</caption>
                <image width="55%">
                    <shortdescription>Triangle OAB with arrow a from O to A and arrow b from O to B. M is the midpoint of OA and N the midpoint of OB. The segment MN is parallel to AB and half as long.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1, >=stealth]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0); \coordinate (A) at (1.5,3.5); \coordinate (B) at (5,0.5);
\coordinate (M) at (0.75,1.75); \coordinate (N) at (2.5,0.25);
\draw[thick,->] (O) -- node[left] {$\mathbf{a}$} (A);
\draw[thick,->] (O) -- node[below] {$\mathbf{b}$} (B);
\draw[thick] (A) -- (B);
\draw[thick,dashed] (M) -- (N);
\foreach \p/\n/\pos in {O/O/below left, A/A/above, B/B/right, M/M/left, N/N/below}
  {\fill (\p) circle (1.5pt) node[\pos] {$\n$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Write <m>\overrightarrow{OM}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{ON}</m> in terms of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m>.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\overrightarrow{OM} = \frac{1}{2}\mathbf{a}</m>, <m>\overrightarrow{ON} = \frac{1}{2}\mathbf{b}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Find <m>\overrightarrow{MN}</m>.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\overrightarrow{MN} = \overrightarrow{ON} - \overrightarrow{OM} = \frac{1}{2}\mathbf{b} - \frac{1}{2}\mathbf{a}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Find <m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m>.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\overrightarrow{AB} = \mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>What do (b) and (c) tell you about the lines <m>MN</m> and <m>AB</m>?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\overrightarrow{MN} = \frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}) = \frac{1}{2}\overrightarrow{AB}</m>: <m>MN</m> is parallel to <m>AB</m> and half as long.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Measure <m>MN</m> and <m>AB</m> in your own triangle. Does your drawing agree?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Every triangle gives <m>MN = \frac{1}{2}AB</m>, up to measuring error, with <m>MN</m> parallel to <m>AB</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Now let <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> be one third of the way from <m>O</m> to <m>A</m> and from <m>O</m> to <m>B</m>. What is <m>\overrightarrow{PQ}</m>?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\overrightarrow{PQ} = \frac{1}{3}\mathbf{b} - \frac{1}{3}\mathbf{a} = \frac{1}{3}\overrightarrow{AB}</m>: parallel to <m>AB</m> and a third as long.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise the strategy for vector proofs in geometry and the rule for equating coefficients.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> the activity chose two base vectors <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m> and wrote every other vector in terms of them.</p></li>
                <li><p><q>Parallel</q> means one vector is a scalar multiple of the other; <q>collinear</q> adds a shared point.</p></li>
                <li><p>Equate coefficients only when the base vectors are not zero and not parallel. A proof is a clear argument written for someone else: state which vectors are parallel, and why.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-proving-with-vectors">
        <title>Proving with Vectors</title>
        <p>
            To prove a result in geometry with vectors:
            <ol>
                <li><p>Choose two base vectors, such as <m>\mathbf{a} = \overrightarrow{OA}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b} = \overrightarrow{OB}</m>, that are not parallel.</p></li>
                <li><p>Write every vector you need in terms of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m>, using ratios along lines and <q>end minus start</q>.</p></li>
                <li><p>To show two lines are parallel, show one vector is a number times the other. To show points are collinear, also show the vectors share a point.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
            <term>Equating coefficients.</term> If <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m> are not zero and not parallel, and
            <md>h\mathbf{a} + k\mathbf{b} = p\mathbf{a} + q\mathbf{b},</md>
            then <m>h = p</m> and <m>k = q</m>. The reason: otherwise <m>(h - p)\mathbf{a} = (q - k)\mathbf{b}</m> would make <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m> parallel. Since <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m> point in different directions, neither can make up for the other.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <figure xml:id="fig-midpoint-theorem-interactive">
        <caption>Drag <m>O</m>, <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> to change the triangle. <m>M</m> and <m>N</m> stay at the midpoints of <m>OA</m> and <m>OB</m>, and the text compares <m>MN</m> with <m>AB</m>.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-midpoint-theorem" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/midpoint-theorem.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-midpoint-theorem" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
            <instructions>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Drag <alert>A</alert> and <alert>B</alert> to make a long thin triangle. Is <m>MN</m> still half of <m>AB</m>?</p></li>
                        <li><p>Drag <alert>O</alert> to the other side of <m>AB</m>. Do the vectors <m>\overrightarrow{MN}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m> still point the same way?</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-parallelogram-diagonals">
        <title>Diagonals of a Parallelogram</title>
        <statement>
            <p><m>OABC</m> is a parallelogram with <m>\overrightarrow{OA} = \mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{OC} = \mathbf{c}</m>. Prove that its diagonals <m>OB</m> and <m>AC</m> bisect each other.</p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-parallelogram-diagonals">
                <caption>Parallelogram <m>OABC</m> with diagonals <m>OB</m> and <m>AC</m>.</caption>
                <image width="55%">
                    <shortdescription>Parallelogram OABC with OA = a along the bottom and OC = c up the left side. The diagonals OB and AC cross at their common midpoint.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1, >=stealth]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0); \coordinate (A) at (4,0.5); \coordinate (C) at (1.2,2.5); \coordinate (B) at (5.2,3);
\draw[thick,->] (O) -- node[below] {$\mathbf{a}$} (A);
\draw[thick,->] (O) -- node[left] {$\mathbf{c}$} (C);
\draw[thick] (A) -- (B) -- (C);
\draw[dashed] (O) -- (B); \draw[dashed] (A) -- (C);
\foreach \p/\n/\pos in {O/O/below left, A/A/below right, B/B/above right, C/C/above left}
  {\fill (\p) circle (1.5pt) node[\pos] {$\n$};}
\fill (2.6,1.5) circle (1.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <m>\overrightarrow{OB} = \overrightarrow{OA} + \overrightarrow{AB} = \mathbf{a} + \mathbf{c}</m>, since <m>\overrightarrow{AB} = \overrightarrow{OC}</m>. So the midpoint of <m>OB</m> has position vector <m>\frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{c})</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>\overrightarrow{AC} = \mathbf{c} - \mathbf{a}</m>, so the midpoint of <m>AC</m> has position vector <m>\mathbf{a} + \frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{c} - \mathbf{a}) = \frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{c})</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                The two midpoints have the same position vector, so they are the same point: the diagonals meet at the midpoint of each, and bisect each other.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-line-parallel-to-a-side">
        <title>A Line Parallel to a Side</title>
        <statement>
            <p>In triangle <m>OAB</m>, <m>\overrightarrow{OA} = \mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{OB} = \mathbf{b}</m>. <m>P</m> is on <m>OA</m> with <m>OP : PA = 2 : 1</m> and <m>Q</m> is on <m>OB</m> with <m>OQ : QB = 2 : 1</m>. Show that <m>PQ</m> is parallel to <m>AB</m> and find the ratio of their lengths.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <m>\overrightarrow{OP} = \frac{2}{3}\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{OQ} = \frac{2}{3}\mathbf{b}</m>, so
                <md>\overrightarrow{PQ} = \frac{2}{3}\mathbf{b} - \frac{2}{3}\mathbf{a} = \frac{2}{3}(\mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}) = \frac{2}{3}\overrightarrow{AB}.</md>
                <m>\overrightarrow{PQ}</m> is a positive multiple of <m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m>, so <m>PQ</m> is parallel to <m>AB</m>, and <m>PQ : AB = 2 : 3</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-where-two-lines-meet">
        <title>Where Two Lines Meet</title>
        <statement>
            <p>In triangle <m>OAB</m>, <m>\overrightarrow{OA} = \mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{OB} = \mathbf{b}</m>. <m>C</m> is on <m>OA</m> with <m>OC : CA = 2 : 1</m>, and <m>D</m> is on <m>AB</m> with <m>AD : DB = 1 : 2</m>. The lines <m>OD</m> and <m>BC</m> meet at <m>X</m>. Find the ratios <m>OX : XD</m> and <m>BX : XC</m>.</p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-where-two-lines-meet">
                <caption>Triangle <m>OAB</m> with the lines <m>OD</m> and <m>BC</m> crossing at <m>X</m>.</caption>
                <image width="55%">
                    <shortdescription>Triangle OAB. C is two thirds of the way from O to A; D is one third of the way from A to B. The lines OD and BC cross at X.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1, >=stealth]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0); \coordinate (A) at (1.5,4); \coordinate (B) at (6,0);
\coordinate (C) at (1,2.667); \coordinate (D) at (3,2.667); \coordinate (X) at (2.25,2);
\draw[thick,->] (O) -- node[left] {$\mathbf{a}$} (A);
\draw[thick,->] (O) -- node[below] {$\mathbf{b}$} (B);
\draw[thick] (A) -- (B);
\draw[dashed] (O) -- (D); \draw[dashed] (B) -- (C);
\foreach \p/\n/\pos in {O/O/below left, A/A/above, B/B/right, C/C/left, D/D/above right, X/X/below}
  {\fill (\p) circle (1.5pt) node[\pos] {$\n$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 1: write the vectors.</alert> By the ratio theorem, <m>\overrightarrow{OD} = \dfrac{2\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}}{3}</m>. Also <m>\overrightarrow{OC} = \frac{2}{3}\mathbf{a}</m>, so <m>\overrightarrow{BC} = \frac{2}{3}\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{b}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 2: two routes to X.</alert> <m>X</m> is on <m>OD</m>, so <m>\overrightarrow{OX} = h\overrightarrow{OD} = \frac{2h}{3}\mathbf{a} + \frac{h}{3}\mathbf{b}</m> for some number <m>h</m>. <m>X</m> is also on <m>BC</m>, so <m>\overrightarrow{OX} = \overrightarrow{OB} + k\overrightarrow{BC} = \frac{2k}{3}\mathbf{a} + (1 - k)\mathbf{b}</m> for some number <m>k</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 3: equate coefficients.</alert> <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m> are not parallel, so
                <md>
                    <mrow>\mathbf{a}:\amp\quad \frac{2h}{3} = \frac{2k}{3}, \text{ so } h = k</mrow>
                    <mrow>\mathbf{b}:\amp\quad \frac{h}{3} = 1 - k = 1 - h, \text{ so } h = \frac{3}{4}</mrow>
                </md>
                Hence <m>h = k = \frac{3}{4}</m>. <m>\overrightarrow{OX} = \frac{3}{4}\overrightarrow{OD}</m>, so <m>OX : XD = 3 : 1</m>; and <m>\overrightarrow{BX} = \frac{3}{4}\overrightarrow{BC}</m>, so <m>BX : XC = 3 : 1</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <paragraphs xml:id="paragraphs-vectors-around-us">
        <title>Vectors Around Us</title>
        <p>
            At the start of this chapter we asked: <em>how do we use vectors to describe positions and movements in space?</em> You can now answer with three numbers for every position and every movement, and the rules for adding, scaling, measuring and dividing them.
        </p>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li><p>A drone spraying a shamba, or an aircraft approaching an airport, has a position and a velocity in three dimensions. Its flight computer adds displacement vectors many times a second.</p></li>
                <li><p>Structural engineers treat the forces in a roof truss or a mast cable as vectors; a roof stands when the force vectors at each joint add to the zero vector.</p></li>
                <li><p>Computer games and 3D animation move every point of a character by adding a vector, and make it larger or smaller with a scalar multiple.</p></li>
                <li><p>A GPS receiver works out its position in three dimensions from the distances to several satellites, using the magnitude formula of <xref ref="sec-magnitude-of-a-vector-in-three-dimensions"/>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Navigation by bearings and distances, in <xref ref="ch-navigation"/>, uses the same ideas in the plane.</p></li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        <p>
            Which of these uses could you see in your own county? Discuss with a partner, and explain one of them to the class in your own words.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Curriculum Links</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Key Inquiry Question</title><p>How do we use vectors to describe positions and movements in space? (Revisited in <xref ref="paragraphs-vectors-around-us" text="title"/>.)</p></li>
            <li><title>Core Competencies</title><p>Self-efficacy, through the graded exercises and the derivation of the ratio theorem; digital literacy, through the interactive figures and the spreadsheet in <xref ref="sec-column-vectors-in-terms-of-unit-vectors"/>.</p></li>
            <li><title>Values</title><p>Unity, through group work in the measuring activities and through comparing everyone's triangle in <xref ref="act-midpoint-theorem"/>.</p></li>
            <li><title>Pertinent and Contemporary Issues</title><p>Effective communication: writing a proof as a clear argument for someone else, and explaining a mistake in another person's working.</p></li>
            <li><title>Learning Outcome</title><p>This lesson carries the outcome on reflecting on the use of vectors in real life (outcome (i) of the sub-strand).</p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

