    <exercises xml:id="exercises-vectors-in-geometry">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-parallelogram-vectors">
            <statement><p><m>OABC</m> is a parallelogram with <m>\overrightarrow{OA} = \mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{OC} = \mathbf{c}</m>, and <m>M</m> is the midpoint of <m>AB</m>. Write <m>\overrightarrow{OB}</m>, <m>\overrightarrow{AC}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{OM}</m> in terms of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{c}</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{c}</m>; <m>\mathbf{c} - \mathbf{a}</m>; <m>\mathbf{a} + \frac{1}{2}\mathbf{c}</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-triangle-median-point">
            <statement><p>In triangle <m>OAB</m>, <m>\overrightarrow{OA} = \mathbf{a}</m>, <m>\overrightarrow{OB} = \mathbf{b}</m> and <m>M</m> is the midpoint of <m>AB</m>. <m>G</m> divides <m>OM</m> internally in the ratio <m>2 : 1</m>. Find <m>\overrightarrow{OM}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{OG}</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b})</m>; <m>\frac{1}{3}(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b})</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-trapezium">
            <statement>
                <p><m>KLMN</m> is a trapezium with <m>\overrightarrow{KL} = \mathbf{p}</m>, <m>\overrightarrow{KN} = \mathbf{q}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{NM} = 3\mathbf{p}</m>. <m>X</m> is on <m>NM</m> with <m>NX : XM = 2 : 1</m>.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Find <m>\overrightarrow{LM}</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find <m>\overrightarrow{KX}</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>What do your answers tell you about the lines <m>KX</m> and <m>LM</m>?</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer><p>(a) <m>2\mathbf{p} + \mathbf{q}</m> (b) <m>2\mathbf{p} + \mathbf{q}</m> (c) <m>KX</m> and <m>LM</m> are parallel and the same length.</p></answer>
            <solution><p>(a) <m>\overrightarrow{LM} = \overrightarrow{LK} + \overrightarrow{KN} + \overrightarrow{NM} = -\mathbf{p} + \mathbf{q} + 3\mathbf{p} = 2\mathbf{p} + \mathbf{q}</m>. (b) <m>\overrightarrow{NX} = \frac{2}{3}\overrightarrow{NM} = 2\mathbf{p}</m>, so <m>\overrightarrow{KX} = \mathbf{q} + 2\mathbf{p}</m>. (c) <m>\overrightarrow{KX} = \overrightarrow{LM}</m>: equal vectors, so <m>KX</m> is parallel to <m>LM</m> and the same length. (So <m>KLMX</m> is a parallelogram.)</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-paths-in-a-plot">
            <statement><p>A farmer near Kitale has a triangular plot. Paths join the midpoints of the three sides. Show that each path is parallel to one side of the plot and half as long.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>The midpoint theorem, applied three times.</p></answer>
            <solution><p>Call the corners <m>O</m>, <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, with <m>\overrightarrow{OA} = \mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{OB} = \mathbf{b}</m>, and let <m>M</m>, <m>N</m>, <m>L</m> be the midpoints of <m>OA</m>, <m>OB</m>, <m>AB</m>. As in <xref ref="act-midpoint-theorem"/>, <m>\overrightarrow{MN} = \frac{1}{2}\overrightarrow{AB}</m>. Also <m>\overrightarrow{OL} = \frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b})</m>, so <m>\overrightarrow{ML} = \frac{1}{2}\mathbf{b} = \frac{1}{2}\overrightarrow{OB}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{NL} = \frac{1}{2}\mathbf{a} = \frac{1}{2}\overrightarrow{OA}</m>. Each path is half of the side it does not touch, and parallel to it.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-find-h-and-k">
            <statement><p>In triangle <m>OAB</m>, <m>\overrightarrow{OA} = \mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{OB} = \mathbf{b}</m>. <m>P</m> is the midpoint of <m>OA</m> and <m>Q</m> is on <m>AB</m> with <m>AQ : QB = 2 : 1</m>. The lines <m>BP</m> and <m>OQ</m> meet at <m>X</m>. Find <m>OX : XQ</m> and <m>BX : XP</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>OX : XQ = 3 : 1</m>; <m>BX : XP = 1 : 1</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>\overrightarrow{OQ} = \frac{1}{3}(\mathbf{a} + 2\mathbf{b})</m>, so <m>\overrightarrow{OX} = h\overrightarrow{OQ} = \frac{h}{3}\mathbf{a} + \frac{2h}{3}\mathbf{b}</m>. <m>\overrightarrow{BP} = \frac{1}{2}\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{b}</m>, so <m>\overrightarrow{OX} = \mathbf{b} + k\overrightarrow{BP} = \frac{k}{2}\mathbf{a} + (1 - k)\mathbf{b}</m>. Equating coefficients: <m>\frac{h}{3} = \frac{k}{2}</m> and <m>\frac{2h}{3} = 1 - k</m>, so <m>k = 1 - k</m>, <m>k = \frac{1}{2}</m> and <m>h = \frac{3}{4}</m>. So <m>OX : XQ = 3 : 1</m> and <m>BX : XP = 1 : 1</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-varignon">
            <title>Challenge: Midpoints of a Quadrilateral</title>
            <statement><p>Prove that the midpoints of the four sides of any quadrilateral <m>ABCD</m> are the corners of a parallelogram.</p></statement>
            <hint><p>Compare each side of the inner shape with a diagonal of <m>ABCD</m>.</p></hint>
            <answer><p>Each side of the inner shape is half of a diagonal: two sides are <m>\frac{1}{2}\overrightarrow{AC}</m> and two are <m>\frac{1}{2}\overrightarrow{BD}</m>.</p></answer>
            <solution><p>Let the midpoints of <m>AB</m>, <m>BC</m>, <m>CD</m>, <m>DA</m> be <m>E</m>, <m>F</m>, <m>G</m>, <m>H</m>, with position vectors <m>\mathbf{e} = \frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b})</m> and so on. Then <m>\overrightarrow{EF} = \mathbf{f} - \mathbf{e} = \frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{c} - \mathbf{a}) = \frac{1}{2}\overrightarrow{AC}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{HG} = \mathbf{g} - \mathbf{h} = \frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{c} - \mathbf{a})</m>. So <m>\overrightarrow{EF} = \overrightarrow{HG}</m>: one pair of opposite sides is equal and parallel, and <m>EFGH</m> is a parallelogram. (In the same way <m>\overrightarrow{FG} = \overrightarrow{EH} = \frac{1}{2}\overrightarrow{BD}</m>.)</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-vectors-in-geometry">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give a <q>vector ledger</q>: a table listing each vector in the figure with a space for it in terms of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m>. Focus on the activity and <xref ref="example-parallelogram-diagonals"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-parallelogram-vectors"/> and <xref ref="ex-triangle-median-point"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-parallelogram-vectors"/> to <xref ref="ex-find-h-and-k"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-varignon"/>. Extension: in <xref ref="example-where-two-lines-meet"/>, find <m>OX : XD</m> when <m>D</m> is the midpoint of <m>AB</m> instead. (Answer: <m>h = \frac{4}{5}</m>, so <m>OX : XD = 4 : 1</m>.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

